<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/foundational/epistemology">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an early lesson in Philosophy and it needs no prior philosophy at all: every
    position is introduced from scratch, and the only equipment required is the willingness to
    ask "but how do you know that?" — and to mean it. The questions are ones you already face
    in courtrooms, clinics and news feeds; here they are made precise.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>distinguish the three kinds of knowledge and say why the classic analysis targets only one of them;</li>
    <li>state the tripartite analysis — knowledge as justified true belief — and the failure each condition excludes;</li>
    <li>explain what a Gettier case is and why it shattered a 2,000-year-old definition;</li>
    <li>compare the post-Gettier repairs — no false lemmas, infallibilism, reliabilism, epistemic virtue — and their costs;</li>
    <li>name the Agrippa trilemma's three endings and the classical sources debate they frame.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Epistemology asks <strong>what knowledge is and where it comes from</strong> — how we
    know anything, from the existence of the external world to the accuracy of a news
    report. It looks like the most abstract corner of philosophy, yet the questions are
    ones you already face: a court must decide when evidence justifies conviction; a doctor
    must decide when a test result justifies treatment; you must decide when a headline
    justifies belief. Epistemology is the discipline that makes those judgements precise —
    and its central analysis (knowledge = justified true belief) stood for 2,000 years
    until a three-page 1963 paper broke it, with the repairs still under construction. The
    story of that analysis, its collapse and its repairs is this lesson, and it is the best
    demonstration in philosophy of how a precise question, a good definition and a wicked
    counterexample work together.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the analysis, the Gettier explosion, scepticism, and the modern repair" widget="equationStep" config={epiCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps tell this lesson's whole story in miniature:
    the 2,000-year analysis (knowledge = justified true belief), the three-page 1963 paper that
    shattered it, the sceptical question of what justifies trusting perception, memory and
    induction at all, and Goldman's reliabilist repair — knowledge from a reliable process.
    Each step gets its own section below.
  </p>

  <h2>Three kinds of knowledge</h2>
  <p>
    "Know" covers three different relations, and the classic analysis targets only one of
    them. <strong>Knowledge by acquaintance</strong> is direct experiential contact — you
    know the smell of coffee, your friend's face, this room. <strong>Knowledge-how</strong>
    (ability knowledge) is competence — you know how to ride a bicycle, swim, factor a
    quadratic, often without being able to state any propositions about it (a gymnast may
    know how, and say nothing). <strong>Propositional knowledge</strong> — knowledge
    <em>that</em> — is knowing that a claim is true: that Paris is the capital of France,
    that the train leaves at nine, that the earth orbits the sun. Only propositional
    knowledge can be true or false, only it can be justified by argument, and only it is
    the battleground below — though the three interpenetrate (you cannot know how to swim
    by acquaintance alone; courts ask whether a witness knew <em>that</em>, not whether
    they knew <em>how</em>).
  </p>

  <h2>The nature of definition</h2>
  <p>
    Before analysing knowledge, epistemology asks what a definition <em>is</em> — and
    Linda Zagzebski's work (1994, 1999) made this explicit. A real definition states the
    conditions that are <strong>necessary and sufficient</strong> for the thing —
    conditions X such that something counts as knowledge <em>if and only if</em> it has X
    — and a good analysis is tested by counterexamples: a single case with all the stated
    conditions and without the thing (or vice versa) refutes it, exactly as a
    counterexample refutes "all swans are white". Zagzebski also proved a deep structural
    worry: <strong>any definition built from internally accessible conditions plus truth
    can be "Gettiered"</strong> — because a lucky accident can rescue the truth of a
    badly grounded belief, whatever extra internal condition you add. That impossibility
    result reframed the whole post-1963 debate: tinkering with conditions looked doomed,
    and externalist definitions (below) became the serious alternative. Defining
    knowledge, it turns out, is the same game as defining water — find the underlying
    essence, not a synonym list — except the "samples" are episodes of knowing, and every
    episode can be rigged.
  </p>

  <h2>The tripartite view: justified true belief</h2>
  <p>
    Plato's <em>Theaetetus</em> proposed the analysis that stood as orthodoxy until 1963:
    you know proposition P when three conditions hold. <strong>Belief:</strong> you
    actually accept P (a fact you have never heard cannot be known by you).
    <strong>Truth:</strong> P must actually be true (a false belief, however confident,
    is not knowledge — the geocentrists believed, but did not know). <strong>Justification:</strong>
    you must have good grounds — evidence, reasons — and not merely guess: a believer who
    wins the lottery by a hunch does not know the winning number. Each clause earns its
    place by excluding a specific failure: no belief (indifference), no truth (error),
    no justification (luck). Worked example: you know the bridge is closed <em>if</em> you
    read the official notice (justification) <em>that</em> the bridge is closed (truth)
    <em>and</em> believe it (belief). Elegant, intuitive, textbook — and wrong.
  </p>

  <h2>Gettier's counterexamples: true belief, justified — but lucky</h2>
  <p>
    In 1963 Edmund Gettier published a three-page paper with cases satisfying all three
    conditions that no one would call knowledge. The widget's version: you see your
    colleague Jones come out of the office counting ten coins, and you are justified in
    believing "Jones will get the job, and Jones has ten coins" — which logically implies
    "the person who will get the job has ten coins." But unknown to you, <em>you</em> get
    the job — and you, coincidentally, also have ten coins. Your belief in the implied
    proposition is true (you have ten coins and got the job), justified (inferred from
    good evidence), believed — and yet it is true only <em>by luck</em>: the evidence that
    justified it points at Jones, and Jones is irrelevant to its truth. The belief's truth
    has come apart from its justification, and when they come apart by accident, we
    intuit: no knowledge. The deeper diagnosis — Zagzebski's — is that <strong>Gettier
    cases are sandwiches of two failures</strong>: a first bit of bad luck (Jones doesn't
    get the job) masked by a second bit of good luck (you coincidentally have ten coins),
    with the justification laundered in between. The luck gap between justification and
    truth is the thing every repair below is trying to close.
  </p>

  <h2>The post-Gettier repairs</h2>
  <p>
    Four families of response, each a serious position with costs. <strong>No false
    lemmas:</strong> add the condition that the justification must not depend on any false
    belief (yours depended on "Jones will get the job" — false). It kills the original
    cases but not refined ones (a case can be Gettiered with no false premise anywhere),
    so it is now regarded as too narrow. <strong>Infallibilism:</strong> demand
    justification so strong it guarantees truth — knowledge requires certainty. This
    closes every luck gap by force, at the catastrophic price of admitting almost
    nothing is known (Descartes' circle problem returns); most epistemologists find the
    price too high, though it clarifies the stakes. <strong>Reliabilism</strong>
    (Goldman, 1979): drop internal justification as the essential ingredient — a true
    belief counts as knowledge if it was produced by a <em>reliable process</em> (normal
    vision, sound reasoning, calibrated instruments). Gettier cases fail because the
    process (inference through a false belief) was unreliable on that occasion. The
    externalism is the point and the controversy: you can know something by a reliable
    process without knowing <em>that</em> the process is reliable — knowledge without
    internally accessible justification. <strong>Epistemic virtue</strong> (Sosa, Zagzebski):
    knowledge is true belief arising from intellectual <em>virtues</em> — the
    well-functioning faculties and habits (careful observation, fair-mindedness) of a good
    knower; Gettier cases fail because luck, not virtue, produced the truth. It humanises
    reliabilism and links epistemology to ethics of belief — but inherits the same
    externalist debates.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the Gettier problem shows knowledge doesn't exist / can't
    be defined." It shows something sharper and more useful: that <em>internal justification
    alone</em> cannot bridge the gap to truth — some factor touching the world (reliability,
    virtue, anti-luck conditions) must be built in. The analysis moved outward, not away.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "a justified belief must be certain, and if certainty
    is impossible, justification is impossible." Justification comes in degrees — the
    court's "beyond reasonable doubt" and the doctor's "preponderance of evidence" are
    thresholds, not guarantees. Demanding certainty first (infallibilism) makes knowledge
    vanish wholesale; the working alternative is calibrating how good the grounds must be
    for which stakes — the move statistics and medicine make daily.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the tripartite analysis gave knowledge three conditions — belief,
    truth, justification — and Gettier showed a belief can satisfy all three and still be true
    only by luck. The repairs either tighten the internal conditions (no false lemmas,
    infallibilism) or move the essential ingredient outward into the world (reliabilism,
    epistemic virtue). The sceptic now asks what justifies the whole enterprise.
  </p>

  <h2>Scepticism and the sources of knowledge</h2>
  <p>
    The repairs assume we know something; the sceptic asks what justifies the assumption.
    <strong>Descartes</strong> doubted everything doubtable — perception (senses err),
    memory, even the external world (the dream and demon arguments) — arriving at one
    unshakable base: <em>I think, therefore I am</em>, and then rebuilding outward.
    <strong>G.E. Moore</strong> answered centuries later with common-sense defiance:
    raising a hand — "here is one hand; therefore an external world exists" — more
    certain, he argued, than any premise used against it. The modern serious version of
    the question is narrower and harder: what justifies trusting <em>perception, memory
    and induction at all</em>? Perception is answered by its reliability; memory by
    continuity of the process; induction famously resists non-circular justification
    (Hume's problem, taken up in the philosophy-of-science lesson) — which is why
    reliabilism's emergence matters practically: the standards by which we trust surveys,
    instruments and now AI systems <em>are</em> applied reliabilism.
  </p>
  <p>
    Beneath the sources sits the oldest problem of all, the <strong>Agrippa
    trilemma</strong>: ask of any justification "and what justifies <em>that</em>?" and
    only three endings exist. The chain of reasons runs back forever
    (<strong>infinitism</strong> — almost nobody believes this); it circles back to
    claims already relied on (<strong>coherentism</strong> — justification as mutual
    support, a web rather than a tower); or it stops at bedrock claims accepted without
    further proof (<strong>foundationalism</strong> — Descartes' search for the
    unshakable base, Moore's hand). Every epistemologist since antiquity has paid one of
    those three prices, and the choice shapes everything downstream: how much science
    can establish, what a court may presume, when an argument is allowed to rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic sources debate frames it historically. <strong>Empiricists</strong> (Locke,
    Hume): knowledge comes from experience; the mind starts as a blank-ish page. Hume
    pushed to the edge: even causation is habit, not observation.
    <strong>Rationalists</strong> (Descartes, Leibniz): some knowledge is built into the
    mind's structure. <strong>Kant's synthesis</strong> (1781): experience itself requires
    the mind's categories — space, time, causality — built in. Modern cognitive science
    has ruled on parts of it: infants arrive with expectations (object permanence,
    number-sense) — core knowledge exists, and Kant looks better than blank-slate
    empiricism did.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "second-hand knowledge isn't really knowledge."
    Testimony delivers nearly everything you know that you did not observe — history,
    geography, the existence of atoms, the contents of other continents. It is a
    genuinely reliable source when the chain behind it is reliable, which is why the
    social epistemology below treats trusting speakers, institutions and instruments as
    a core epistemic skill, not a second-class shortcut.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> scepticism forces the question of what justifies trusting
    perception, memory and induction at all; every chain of justification ends in the Agrippa
    trilemma's three endings — regress, circle or bedrock; and cognitive science has partly
    settled the empiricist–rationalist debate in Kant's favour. Testimony, meanwhile, delivers
    nearly everything you know that you did not observe.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>State the tripartite analysis of knowledge, and say which specific failure each of its three conditions excludes.</li>
    <li>In the ten-coins case, all three conditions hold — so why is the belief still not knowledge, and what must any repair close?</li>
    <li>What are the Agrippa trilemma's three endings, and which horn do foundationalism and coherentism each accept?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) You know P when P is true, you believe P, and you are
    justified in believing P: belief excludes indifference, truth excludes error, justification
    excludes luck. (2) The belief is true only by luck — the justification points at Jones, and
    Jones is irrelevant to its truth; every repair must close the gap between justification and
    truth that luck exploits (or, on Zagzebski's result, accept that no purely internal
    conditions can). (3) The chain of reasons runs back forever (infinitism), circles back to
    claims already relied on (coherentism) or stops at bedrock claims (foundationalism);
    coherentism embraces the web, foundationalism the unshakable base.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Epistemology asks what knowledge is and where it comes from — and you already make its judgements daily.</li>
    <li>Only propositional knowledge (knowing that) is analysed; acquaintance and know-how are different relations.</li>
    <li>A real definition states necessary and sufficient conditions, and one counterexample refutes it.</li>
    <li>Knowledge as justified true belief stood for 2,000 years; Gettier cases are sandwiches of two failures — bad luck masked by good.</li>
    <li>Zagzebski's result: any definition built from internal conditions plus truth can be Gettiered, so the repairs move outward (reliabilism, epistemic virtue).</li>
    <li>Justification comes in degrees; infallibilism's demand for certainty makes knowledge vanish wholesale.</li>
    <li>Every justification chain ends in the Agrippa trilemma: infinite regress, coherence or bedrock.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    Hume's problem of induction is deferred to the philosophy-of-science lesson, where
    reliabilism returns as the standards by which surveys, instruments and AI systems are
    judged; the Bayesian epistemology met here — justification as conferring high probability
    given the evidence — is what the statistics ladder formalises, and the prosecutor's fallacy
    shows what getting it wrong costs.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The current frontier is <strong>social epistemology</strong>: most of what any
    individual knows — history, science, the geography of places never visited — arrives
    by <em>testimony</em>, so the epistemology of trusting speakers, institutions and
    algorithms is not peripheral but the main event. Zagzebski herself later proposed
    that trust in other knowers is epistemically basic in the way perception is — a
    conscious echo of the reliabilist move at the social scale. Formal work quantifies
    Gettier luck (the "safety" condition: the belief would have been true in nearby
    worlds — the counterfactual repair) and models justification probabilistically
    (Bayesian epistemology), where "justified" means "confers high probability given the
    evidence" — the framework the statistics ladder formalises.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two closing connections. First, the court and the clinic are epistemology with
    deadlines: legal standards ("beyond reasonable doubt", "preponderance of evidence")
    are explicit justification thresholds; diagnostic thresholds for a positive scan are
    reliabilism applied to an instrument. Second, the AI case is the live experiment: a
    language model outputs true statements by a process whose reliability we assess
    externally, while its internal "justification" is opaque — reliabilism versus
    infallibilism, argued in product reviews. The 2,300-year-old question — what turns
    true belief into knowledge — is currently being run at planetary scale.
  </p>
  <p>
    The courtroom case that made the probability explicit: <strong>the prosecutor's
    fallacy</strong>. In the 1999 trial of Sally Clark — convicted over two cot deaths
    and cleared in 2003 — an expert testified that the chance of two such deaths in one
    family was about 1 in 73 million, a figure that wrongly assumed the deaths
    independent and, worse, inverted the question: what mattered was not the rarity of
    the deaths but the comparison between <em>double murder</em> and <em>natural double
    death</em>. Confusing P(evidence | innocent) with P(innocent | evidence) is a formal
    error with a life sentence attached, and it is precisely the Bayesian epistemology
    above, argued in a courtroom — fine print with liberty riding on it.
  </p>
